# The supply-chain baseline docs/design/03-security.md section 12 names, as a DEFAULT rather than as
# a flag somebody has to remember (issue #87).
#
# Lifecycle scripts are the primary npm attack vector: a postinstall in a compromised transitive
# dependency executes with the credentials of whoever typed the command. Every call site in this
# repository already passes --ignore-scripts — the Makefile's `setup` and `web-deps`,
# scripts/build-web.sh, scripts/gen-client.sh, scripts/test-e2e.sh, deploy/Dockerfile's web stage
# and the `lint / web` CI job — and test/repo/web_lint_test.go proves the flag works. What none of
# that covered is the obvious command:
#
#     cd web && pnpm install        # without this file: runs every dependency's lifecycle scripts
#
# which is what every pnpm tutorial says and what somebody types when a make target fails for an
# unrelated reason. The flags stay: belt and braces, and they keep working from outside the tree.
#
# THIS FILE IS NOT THE ONE THAT COVERS web/. pnpm resolves the project .npmrc from the directory
# holding package.json — it does NOT walk upward — so with pnpm 9.15.9 and no pnpm-workspace.yaml,
# `cd web && pnpm config get ignore-scripts` reads web/.npmrc and never sees this file. Both exist
# and test/repo/npmrc_test.go asserts both, because a root-only file would have been a decoration.
# This one covers a package manager run at the repository root.
#
# If a dependency genuinely needs its lifecycle script, the answer is a reviewed
# `pnpm.onlyBuiltDependencies` entry in the relevant package.json, naming that one package — never
# a blanket re-enable here.
#
# NEVER put a credential in this file. It is committed. Registry auth (`_authToken`, `_auth`,
# `_password`) belongs in ~/.npmrc or an environment variable; npmrc_test.go fails on one here.
ignore-scripts=true
